00:00:20.682 --> 00:00:26.280
Recently, we did an episode about sperm, and
in it we talked about the awkward history

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:30.140
of how, at times, humans have overestimated
the role of sperm.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:34.270
It is true that sperm is essential to human
reproduction.

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:39.480
But the spermist school of thought maintained
that the sperm was where all of the important

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:41.810
information encoding our traits was located.

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:48.250
And the egg, well…the egg was just there
to provide some nourishment.

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:52.060
That point of view was wrong about how genetic
information gets passed along.

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:58.280
But also…it really just underestimated the
engineering marvel that is the egg.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:03.700
Now, it’s easy to focus on eggs in sexual
reproduction because for humans and many other

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:09.409
animals, the fertilization of the egg by a
sperm is the key step that drives the development

00:01:09.409 --> 00:01:10.409
of a new embryo.

00:01:10.409 --> 00:01:18.159
And the meeting of sperm and egg is an incredibly
valuable one, allowing organisms to mix and

00:01:18.159 --> 00:01:26.319
match genes between individuals so that the
population can better evolve and adapt.

00:01:26.319 --> 00:01:30.100
But not all sperm/egg pairings introduce variation.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:37.350
For example, some flatworms are hermaphroditic,
and under certain conditions, they might fertilize

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:38.569
their own eggs.

00:01:38.569 --> 00:01:48.229
In one species, that process involves injecting
sperm directly into their own head.

00:01:54.571 --> 00:02:00.939
But also, while all sperm needs an egg, not
all eggs need sperm.

00:02:00.939 --> 00:02:06.469
There are quite a few organisms, like many
gastrotrichs and rotifers, that reproduce

00:02:06.469 --> 00:02:09.940
by parthenogenesis.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:18.060
In these species, the female makes an unfertilized
egg that develops and eventually hatches into

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:22.170
a miniature version of its singular parent.

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:26.960
Both self-fertilization and parthenogenesis
come along with the advantage that you do

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:31.459
not have to find a mate, you can just go ahead
and get your own egg going.

00:02:31.459 --> 00:02:36.349
And that’s great if you want to grow a large
population or there just aren’t very many

00:02:36.349 --> 00:02:44.379
viable mating options around, but it does
come at the cost of genetic diversity.

00:02:44.379 --> 00:02:49.390
As an egg develops and the cells within it
divide and differentiate, there is a flurry

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:51.790
of activity to support them.

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:54.190
Developing an organism is an involved process.

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:58.810
It’s not like you have all your materials
laid out in front of you, and all that’s

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:00.840
left is to assemble those pieces.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:06.870
It’s more like starting out with one brick
and being told to make more bricks from it.

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:11.989
And as you make more bricks from those bricks,
you have to also make them more and more specialized

00:03:11.989 --> 00:03:14.370
until you get, like, a castle.

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:18.790
Now, coming up with a comparison inevitably
sells the complexity of the whole process

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:19.790
short.

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:25.269
In rotifer eggs, scientists have found an
uptick in genes involved with cell proliferation,

00:03:25.269 --> 00:03:32.499
adhesion, and communication—all of which
makes sense because there is so much coordination

00:03:32.499 --> 00:03:38.269
needed to make sure that as cells divide,
they’re talking to each other and assembling

00:03:38.269 --> 00:03:45.299
themselves correctly into one of the most
complex structures the universe has ever seen,

00:03:45.299 --> 00:03:49.400
a living, multicellular organism.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:54.791
Now, in addition to everything going on inside
the egg, there’s everything going on in

00:03:54.791 --> 00:03:56.700
the world around it.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:02.879
An egg that has been released into the microcosmos
is subject to the environmental whims of its

00:04:02.879 --> 00:04:07.969
surroundings, which makes it all the more
remarkable how many animals produce eggs that

00:04:07.969 --> 00:04:15.829
are crafted to wait and survive those conditions
for long periods of time.

00:04:15.829 --> 00:04:21.380
Tardigrades, for example, can produce eggs
capable of withstanding extreme heat and extreme

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:26.980
cold, as well as doses of radiation that would
kill a human being.

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:30.350
Those eggs however are different from the
ones that are currently on your screen, which

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:34.360
are hydrated and thus not able to survive
those extremes.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:39.640
They’re only good at surviving these wild
circumstances when they’ve been desiccated,

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:45.360
or dried out.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:51.110
Those hardy eggs are called dormant or “resting”
eggs, which makes them sound inert.

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:55.200
And that’s kind of the point: the eggs are
inactive.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:05:02.789
Their metabolism essentially shuts down, as
does synthesis of any new DNA, RNA, or proteins.

00:05:02.789 --> 00:05:07.530
What the eggs take with them, going into that
state is all that they have to survive, like

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:09.630
an internal desert island.

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:14.660
But it takes a lot of work to make that inactivity
possible.

00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:20.110
There are a lot of animals that produce dormant
eggs, and the conditions that drive that dormancy

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:25.260
and the way they emerge from it vary according
to that animals’ lifestyle and environment.

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:30.280
For many rotifer species, resting eggs are
the result of sexual reproduction6.

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:35.590
And an important thing for us to note as you
look at these rotifer eggs: there are morphological

00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:42.500
features specific to rotifer resting eggs,
but we are not familiar enough with those

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:47.630
traits to be able to make the call on whether
or not the eggs we’re looking at are resting

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:48.630
or non-resting.

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:53.830
Now, if we had some decades, we might be able
to tell you because that’s how long some

00:05:53.830 --> 00:05:56.370
resting eggs can survive.

00:05:56.370 --> 00:06:04.740
Some have even hatched after over 100 years
of rest, but alas we definitely do not have

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:11.810
that long to wait.

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:15.960
Even if we’re having a hard time telling
resting and non-resting eggs apart from the

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:20.560
outside, they are very different on the inside.

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:25.130
These protections can be straightforward,
like an abundance of proteins that protect

00:06:25.130 --> 00:06:27.970
the egg from heat and oxidative stress.

00:06:27.970 --> 00:06:33.830
But on top of protecting cells from external
factors, the resting egg has to be protected

00:06:33.830 --> 00:06:35.780
from itself.

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:42.130
In animals, apoptosis--or programmed cell
death-- is a necessary part of our survival.

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:46.930
It helps our bodies clear out cells that are
no longer healthy or necessary.

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:50.110
Without it, you and I would have already died
of cancer.

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:57.460
But a cell in the dessicated, dormant environment
of the resting egg is not going to look very healthy.

00:06:57.460 --> 00:07:03.370
So some animals like Artemia have devised
molecular strategies that interfere with the

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:09.100
pathways that lead to apoptosis so that the
cell can be left alive

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:15.620
And alongside the protections are stores of
messenger RNA, also kept at rest and stabilized

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:18.060
while the egg is in stasis.

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:23.800
Those stores of RNA contain the instructions
the egg needs to move quickly when it emerges

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:25.410
from its resting state.

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:32.410
Because, after decades or even a century of
waiting, once it’s time to get going, it’s

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:37.330
TIME TO GET GOING and those instructions will
allow the egg to jump start the synthesis

00:07:37.330 --> 00:07:42.780
of the proteins that it will need to grow
and divide and bring new life back to the

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:56.270
microcosmos wherever they have ended up.

